# TypeScript Standards (Shared)

## Naming

| Element | Convention | Example |
|---------|------------|---------|
| Variables, functions, params | `camelCase`, English | `fetchUserById` |
| Classes, types, interfaces | `PascalCase` | `UserRepository` |
| Global constants | `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` when truly constant | `MAX_RETRY_COUNT` |
| Files | kebab-case or project convention | `user-service.ts` |

Avoid obscure abbreviations unless established in the project (`req`, `res` in handlers OK).

## Typing

- Avoid `any`; if unavoidable, comment why and narrow scope.
- Explicit return types on public APIs and use-case boundaries.
- Derive types from schemas (Zod, etc.) when validation exists — single source of truth.
- Centralize shared types in `types/`, `domain/`, or `shared/` per project layout.

## Functions

- Single responsibility; prefer < 40 lines for business logic.
- Extract complex logic to use-cases, services, or hooks — not inline in handlers/components.
- No duplicated logic: propose shared helper when pattern repeats 3+ times.

## Imports

Order:

1. External libraries
2. Internal aliases (`@/...`)
3. Relative imports

Prefer path aliases over deep relative paths (`../../../`).

## Error Handling

- Never swallow errors silently.
- Use project logger; map to user-safe messages at boundaries.
- Async: always `await` with try/catch or `.catch()` at orchestration layer.

## Logging

- No `console.log` in production paths — use configured logger.
- Never log: passwords, tokens, full PII, credit card data.

## Comments

Comment **why**, not **what**. Document non-obvious business rules and edge cases only.